<![CDATA[<h1>MicroPython Function Reference</h1><p>Author: 孤筝(lvbowen040427@163.com)</p>
        
          <p>This article collects the parts of MicroPython&rsquo;s <code>machine</code> module that are most useful on a Raspberry Pi Pico (RP2040). Everything has been checked against the <a href="https://docs.micropython.org/en/latest/library/machine.html">official MicroPython documentation</a>.</p>
<h2 id="machine-hardware-related-functions">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#machine-hardware-related-functions"></a>
machine: Hardware-Related Functions
</h2><p>There are two numbering schemes for pins:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>GPIO number</strong> (GP0–GP29): the one used in code; <code>Pin(25)</code> means GP25.</li>
<li><strong>Physical pin number</strong>: the 40 header positions on the board. These are not the same as GPIO numbers, so be careful when wiring.</li>
</ul>
<h3 id="class-pin">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#class-pin"></a>
class Pin
</h3><h4 id="constructor-machinepinid-mode-1-pull-1-valuenone">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#constructor-machinepinid-mode-1-pull-1-valuenone"></a>
Constructor <code>machine.Pin(id, mode=-1, pull=-1, value=None)</code>
</h4><table>
	<thead>
			<tr>
					<th>Parameter</th>
					<th>Meaning</th>
			</tr>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
			<tr>
					<td><code>id</code></td>
					<td>Pin identifier. On the Pico, 0–29 (GP0–GP29)</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
					<td><code>mode</code></td>
					<td><code>None</code> / <code>Pin.IN</code>(0) / <code>Pin.OUT</code>(1) / <code>Pin.OPEN_DRAIN</code>(2)</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
					<td><code>pull</code></td>
					<td>Internal pull resistor, <strong>valid only in input mode</strong>: <code>None</code> / <code>Pin.PULL_UP</code>(1) / <code>Pin.PULL_DOWN</code>(2)</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
					<td><code>value</code></td>
					<td>Valid only for output/open-drain mode; initial level: 0 low, 1 high</td>
			</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p>Note that it is <code>Pin.PULL_DOWN</code>, not <code>Pin.DOWN</code>.</p>
</blockquote>
<h4 id="pininitmode-1-pull-1-valuenone">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#pininitmode-1-pull-1-valuenone"></a>
<code>Pin.init(mode=-1, pull=-1, value=None)</code>
</h4><p>Re-initialise the pin. Only the arguments you pass are changed; everything else keeps its current state.</p>
<h4 id="pinvaluex">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#pinvaluex"></a>
<code>Pin.value([x])</code>
</h4><ul>
<li>With no argument: returns the current pin level (0 or 1).</li>
<li>With an argument: writes the level. The argument is converted to a boolean (<code>True</code> sets 1, <code>False</code> sets 0).</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="pinon--pinoff-and-pinhigh--pinlow">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#pinon--pinoff-and-pinhigh--pinlow"></a>
<code>Pin.on()</code> / <code>Pin.off()</code> and <code>Pin.high()</code> / <code>Pin.low()</code>
</h4><ul>
<li><code>on()</code>, <code>high()</code>: drive the pin high (1).</li>
<li><code>off()</code>, <code>low()</code>: drive the pin low (0).</li>
</ul>
<p>These four are meaningful only in output/open-drain mode. <code>low()</code> and <code>high()</code> are not part of the core API, but they are available on the Pico (rp2 port).</p>
<h4 id="pintoggle">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#pintoggle"></a>
<code>Pin.toggle()</code>
</h4><p>Flips the output level from 0 to 1 or 1 to 0. <code>toggle()</code> is available on the rp2, esp32, and several other ports; where it is missing, use <code>Pin.value(not Pin.value())</code> instead.</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"><code class="language-python" data-lang="python"><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="kn">from</span> <span class="nn">machine</span> <span class="kn">import</span> <span class="n">Pin</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="kn">import</span> <span class="nn">time</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="n">led</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">Pin</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">25</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Pin</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">OUT</span><span class="p">)</span>   <span class="c1"># the on-board LED of a Pico is GP25</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="k">while</span> <span class="kc">True</span><span class="p">:</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">    <span class="n">led</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">toggle</span><span class="p">()</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">    <span class="n">time</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">sleep</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">1</span><span class="p">)</span>        <span class="c1"># flip once per second</span>
</span></span></code></pre></div><h4 id="pinirqhandlernone-triggerpinirq_falling--pinirq_rising">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#pinirqhandlernone-triggerpinirq_falling--pinirq_rising"></a>
<code>Pin.irq(handler=None, trigger=Pin.IRQ_FALLING | Pin.IRQ_RISING)</code>
</h4><p>Configures an external interrupt. The callback runs whenever the trigger condition occurs.</p>
<ul>
<li><code>handler</code>: the callback, which <strong>must accept one <code>Pin</code> argument</strong>.</li>
<li><code>trigger</code>: the condition, and multiple values can be OR-ed with <code>|</code>:</li>
</ul>
<table>
	<thead>
			<tr>
					<th>Constant</th>
					<th>Trigger</th>
			</tr>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
			<tr>
					<td><code>Pin.IRQ_FALLING</code></td>
					<td>falling edge</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
					<td><code>Pin.IRQ_RISING</code></td>
					<td>rising edge</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
					<td><code>Pin.IRQ_LOW_LEVEL</code></td>
					<td>low level</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
					<td><code>Pin.IRQ_HIGH_LEVEL</code></td>
					<td>high level</td>
			</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"><code class="language-python" data-lang="python"><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="k">def</span> <span class="nf">on_press</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">pin</span><span class="p">):</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">    <span class="nb">print</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s2">&#34;Button pressed&#34;</span><span class="p">)</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="n">button</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">Pin</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">15</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Pin</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">IN</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Pin</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">PULL_UP</span><span class="p">)</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="n">button</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">irq</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">handler</span><span class="o">=</span><span class="n">on_press</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">trigger</span><span class="o">=</span><span class="n">Pin</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">IRQ_FALLING</span><span class="p">)</span>
</span></span></code></pre></div><h3 id="class-pwm">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#class-pwm"></a>
class PWM
</h3><p>PWM (pulse-width modulation) switches a pin on and off rapidly to emulate intermediate voltages. It is used for dimming LEDs, controlling motor speed, and driving servos.</p>
<h4 id="machinepwmpin-freq-duty_u16">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#machinepwmpin-freq-duty_u16"></a>
<code>machine.PWM(pin, freq=..., duty_u16=...)</code>
</h4><p>Creates a PWM output on the given pin; <code>pin</code> is a <code>Pin</code> object.</p>
<h4 id="common-methods">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#common-methods"></a>
Common methods
</h4><table>
	<thead>
			<tr>
					<th>Method</th>
					<th>Purpose</th>
			</tr>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
			<tr>
					<td><code>PWM.freq([value])</code></td>
					<td>get/set the frequency in Hz, roughly 8 Hz – 62.5 MHz (at a 125 MHz CPU clock)</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
					<td><code>PWM.duty_u16([value])</code></td>
					<td>get/set the duty cycle as <strong>0–65535</strong>, the ratio <code>value / 65535</code></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
					<td><code>PWM.duty_ns([value])</code></td>
					<td>get/set the high time within one period, in nanoseconds</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
					<td><code>PWM.deinit()</code></td>
					<td>stop the PWM output</td>
			</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
<blockquote>
<p>The upper bound of <code>duty_u16</code> is <strong>65535</strong>, not 65536. Passing 65536 overflows.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>RP2040 has 8 independent PWM slices with 2 channels each, 16 channels in total. The two channels of a slice <strong>must share a frequency</strong> but may have different duty cycles.</p>
<h4 id="example-breathing-led">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#example-breathing-led"></a>
Example: breathing LED
</h4><div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"><code class="language-python" data-lang="python"><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="kn">from</span> <span class="nn">machine</span> <span class="kn">import</span> <span class="n">Pin</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">PWM</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="kn">import</span> <span class="nn">time</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="n">led</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">PWM</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Pin</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">25</span><span class="p">))</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="n">led</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">freq</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">1000</span><span class="p">)</span>      <span class="c1"># 1 kHz</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="n">duty</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="mi">0</span>            <span class="c1"># duty cycle in percent</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="n">step</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="mi">1</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="k">while</span> <span class="kc">True</span><span class="p">:</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">    <span class="n">duty</span> <span class="o">+=</span> <span class="n">step</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">    <span class="k">if</span> <span class="n">duty</span> <span class="o">&gt;=</span> <span class="mi">100</span><span class="p">:</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">        <span class="n">duty</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="mi">100</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">        <span class="n">step</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="o">-</span><span class="mi">1</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">    <span class="k">elif</span> <span class="n">duty</span> <span class="o">&lt;=</span> <span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">:</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">        <span class="n">duty</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="mi">0</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">        <span class="n">step</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="mi">1</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">    <span class="c1"># convert percent to the 0-65535 range, without overflowing</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">    <span class="n">led</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">duty_u16</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="nb">int</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">duty</span> <span class="o">/</span> <span class="mi">100</span> <span class="o">*</span> <span class="mi">65535</span><span class="p">))</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">    <span class="k">if</span> <span class="n">duty</span> <span class="o">%</span> <span class="mi">5</span> <span class="o">==</span> <span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">:</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">        <span class="nb">print</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">duty</span><span class="p">)</span>  <span class="c1"># convenient for plotting</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">    <span class="n">time</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">sleep</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mf">0.01</span><span class="p">)</span>  <span class="c1"># roughly a 2-second round trip</span>
</span></span></code></pre></div><h4 id="example-button-controlled-led-with-software-debouncing">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#example-button-controlled-led-with-software-debouncing"></a>
Example: button-controlled LED (with software debouncing)
</h4><div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"><code class="language-python" data-lang="python"><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="kn">from</span> <span class="nn">machine</span> <span class="kn">import</span> <span class="n">Pin</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="kn">import</span> <span class="nn">time</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="n">button</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">Pin</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">15</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Pin</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">IN</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Pin</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">PULL_UP</span><span class="p">)</span>   <span class="c1"># reads 0 while pressed</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="n">led_board</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">Pin</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">25</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Pin</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">OUT</span><span class="p">)</span>            <span class="c1"># on-board LED</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="n">led_ext</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">Pin</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">16</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Pin</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">OUT</span><span class="p">)</span>              <span class="c1"># external LED</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="k">while</span> <span class="kc">True</span><span class="p">:</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">    <span class="n">led_ext</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">off</span><span class="p">()</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">    <span class="k">if</span> <span class="n">button</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">value</span><span class="p">()</span> <span class="o">==</span> <span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">:</span>             <span class="c1"># possibly pressed</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">        <span class="n">time</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">sleep</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mf">0.01</span><span class="p">)</span>                <span class="c1"># wait 10 ms and check again to filter bounce</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">        <span class="k">if</span> <span class="n">button</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">value</span><span class="p">()</span> <span class="o">==</span> <span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">:</span>         <span class="c1"># confirmed</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">            <span class="n">led_board</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">toggle</span><span class="p">()</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">            <span class="n">led_ext</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">on</span><span class="p">()</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">            <span class="nb">print</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="s2">&#34;The button is pressed.&#34;</span><span class="p">)</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">            <span class="k">while</span> <span class="n">button</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">value</span><span class="p">()</span> <span class="o">==</span> <span class="mi">0</span><span class="p">:</span>  <span class="c1"># wait for release</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">                <span class="n">time</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">sleep</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mf">0.01</span><span class="p">)</span>
</span></span></code></pre></div><blockquote>
<p>A mechanical button bounces for a few milliseconds when pressed or released, and the CPU can read that as several presses. <strong>Software debouncing</strong> means: after detecting a change, wait about 10 ms and read the pin again to confirm.</p>
</blockquote>
<h3 id="class-adc">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#class-adc"></a>
class ADC
</h3><p>The ADC (analog-to-digital converter) turns an analog voltage into a digital reading.</p>
<h4 id="machineadcid">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#machineadcid"></a>
<code>machine.ADC(id)</code>
</h4><p><code>id</code> may be a <code>Pin</code> object or a channel number:</p>
<ul>
<li>On the Pico, channels <strong>0–3 map to GP26–GP29</strong> (GP29 is wired to VSYS on the board);</li>
<li>Channel <strong>4</strong> is the on-chip temperature sensor, also reachable as <code>ADC.CORE_TEMP</code>.</li>
</ul>
<h4 id="adcread_u16">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#adcread_u16"></a>
<code>ADC.read_u16()</code>
</h4><p>Returns the reading as an integer in <strong>0–65535</strong>, spanning the input range 0–3.3 V.</p>
<div class="highlight"><pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"><code class="language-python" data-lang="python"><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="kn">from</span> <span class="nn">machine</span> <span class="kn">import</span> <span class="n">ADC</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="n">Pin</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl">
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="n">adc</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">ADC</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">Pin</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="mi">26</span><span class="p">))</span>          <span class="c1"># equivalent to ADC(0)</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="n">value</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">adc</span><span class="o">.</span><span class="n">read_u16</span><span class="p">()</span>      <span class="c1"># 0 - 65535</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="n">voltage</span> <span class="o">=</span> <span class="n">value</span> <span class="o">/</span> <span class="mi">65535</span> <span class="o">*</span> <span class="mf">3.3</span>   <span class="c1"># convert to volts</span>
</span></span><span class="line"><span class="cl"><span class="nb">print</span><span class="p">(</span><span class="n">voltage</span><span class="p">,</span> <span class="s2">&#34;V&#34;</span><span class="p">)</span>
</span></span></code></pre></div><blockquote>
<p>The range is 0–65535. The RP2040&rsquo;s ADC hardware is only 12-bit (0–4095); MicroPython scales it linearly to 16 bits, so the lowest few bits carry no real precision.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2 id="summary">
<a class="header-anchor" href="#summary"></a>
Summary
</h2><table>
	<thead>
			<tr>
					<th>Task</th>
					<th>Key point</th>
			</tr>
	</thead>
	<tbody>
			<tr>
					<td>Digital I/O</td>
					<td><code>Pin(id, mode, pull)</code>; read with <code>value()</code>, write with <code>value()</code> / <code>on()</code> / <code>off()</code> / <code>toggle()</code></td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
					<td>Interrupts</td>
					<td><code>Pin.irq(handler, trigger)</code>; the callback must take one <code>Pin</code> argument</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
					<td>Dimming / speed control</td>
					<td><code>PWM(pin)</code> + <code>freq()</code> + <code>duty_u16()</code>; duty is capped at 65535</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
					<td>Reading analog input</td>
					<td><code>ADC(Pin(26))</code> + <code>read_u16()</code>, returning 0–65535</td>
			</tr>
			<tr>
					<td>Button bounce</td>
					<td>after detecting a change, wait 10 ms and confirm</td>
			</tr>
	</tbody>
</table>

        
